# github-platform-creds Secret — example manifest.
#
# This Secret holds the platform GitHub organization's Personal Access
# Token. The Session API reads it at boot to create per-app repos under
# the platform org for the new-app entry point; the workspace-init init
# container and git-finalizer sidecar reuse the same token for the
# initial seed push and the on-exit commit-and-push.
#
# This is distinct from `git-creds` — that one is for the user-supplied
# import-repo path and is scoped to whichever single test repo the
# operator has authorized. The platform-creds PAT can only create and
# write to repos under the platform org. Keeping them separate respects
# the principle of least privilege; see
# docs/plans/2026-05-12-002-feat-coding-session-scaffold-bootstrap-plan.md
# Key Technical Decisions → "Two GitHub credential sources".
#
# Setup (NOT committed with a real value) — full walkthrough lives in
# docs/runbooks/platform-github-org-setup.md. Two prerequisites trip up
# first-time setup, so they're worth surfacing here:
#
#   (a) The org's PAT policy defaults to "Require administrator
#       approval". Set it to "Do not require administrator approval"
#       in Org Settings → Personal access tokens → Fine-grained tokens
#       BEFORE creating the PAT, otherwise the PAT sits in a Pending
#       state and every API call 403s.
#   (b) The "Repository permissions" section in the PAT-creation form
#       only appears AFTER you select "All repositories" under
#       "Repository access". Set Repository access first, then scroll
#       down to expand Contents → Read and write.
#
# Quick reference of scopes required:
#   - Organization permissions:
#       - Administration: Read and write   (required to create repos)
#   - Repository permissions (applies to ALL repos in the org):
#       - Administration: Read and write   (also required for createInOrg —
#                                           empirically GitHub 403s on
#                                           "Resource not accessible by
#                                           personal access token" without
#                                           this, even though only the
#                                           Organization grant is documented)
#       - Contents: Read and write          (required for clone + push)
#       - Metadata: Read                    (auto-included)
#
# Setup steps:
#   1. Provision the platform GitHub org (one-time).
#   2. Set the org's fine-grained PAT approval policy (one-time).
#   3. Create the fine-grained PAT.
#   4. Copy this file: `cp github-platform-creds-secret.yaml.example github-platform-creds-secret.yaml`
#   5. Replace <YOUR_PAT_HERE> with the token value.
#   6. Update OPENVOID_PLATFORM_GITHUB_ORG in infra/local/session-api.yaml
#      if your org name differs from `openvoid-platform`.
#   7. Apply: `kubectl apply -f github-platform-creds-secret.yaml`
#   8. Verify: `kubectl get secret github-platform-creds -n openvoid-sessions`
#   9. Restart the Session API so it re-reads the Secret at boot:
#        `kubectl rollout restart deployment session-api -n openvoid-system`
#
# DO NOT commit the populated file. `.gitignore` excludes
# `github-platform-creds-secret.yaml` (without the .example suffix).
#
# Rotation: regenerate the PAT in GitHub, update stringData.token, re-apply
# this manifest, and restart the Session API Deployment.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  # Namespace is openvoid-sessions (NOT openvoid-system) so the
  # workspace-init and git-finalizer containers — which run as part of
  # per-session Pods in openvoid-sessions — can mount this Secret via
  # `secretKeyRef`. Kubernetes does not support cross-namespace Secret
  # references; both consumers must live in the same namespace as the
  # Secret. The Session API itself runs in openvoid-system and reads
  # this Secret via the K8s API (RBAC grants `get` on this specific
  # resourceName).
  name: github-platform-creds
  namespace: openvoid-sessions
type: Opaque
stringData:
  token: <YOUR_PAT_HERE>
